DAY 7 - PYTHON STRINGS
=========================

1. STRING BASICS
A string is a sequence of characters written inside single quotes, double
quotes, or triple quotes. Triple quotes can also represent multi-line text.

Python strings are immutable, so their existing characters cannot be changed
directly after creation.

2. COMMON STRING OPERATIONS
- Concatenation (+): combines strings.
- Repetition (*): repeats a string.
- Indexing: accesses a character using its position.
- Slicing: extracts a portion of a string.
- Membership (in / not in): checks whether text occurs inside a string.

3. BUILT-IN FUNCTIONS USED WITH STRINGS
- len()      -> number of characters
- max()      -> character with the highest value
- min()      -> character with the lowest value
- sorted()   -> sorted list of characters
- ord()      -> character to numeric code
- chr()      -> numeric code to character

4. CASE CONVERSION
- upper()      -> converts to uppercase
- lower()      -> converts to lowercase
- capitalize() -> capitalizes the first character
- title()      -> capitalizes each word
- swapcase()   -> changes uppercase to lowercase and vice versa
- casefold()   -> performs stronger lowercase conversion

5. ALIGNMENT AND FORMATTING
- center(width, fillchar) -> centers text
- ljust(width, fillchar)  -> aligns text to the left
- rjust(width, fillchar)  -> aligns text to the right
- zfill(width)            -> adds zeros to the left

6. SEARCHING
- find(sub)   -> first matching position, or -1 when absent
- rfind(sub)  -> last matching position
- index(sub)  -> first matching position; raises an error if absent
- rindex(sub) -> last matching position; raises an error if absent
- count(sub)  -> number of occurrences

7. STRING TESTING
These methods return True or False:
- startswith()
- endswith()
- isalpha()
- isalnum()
- islower()
- isupper()
- isspace()
- istitle()
- isidentifier()

8. NUMERIC CHARACTER TESTS
- isdecimal() -> most restrictive decimal-digit check
- isdigit()   -> accepts additional digit characters such as superscripts
- isnumeric() -> broader numeric-character check, including fractions and
                 Roman numeral characters

9. REPLACE AND TRANSLATE
- replace(old, new) -> substitutes matching text
- maketrans()       -> creates a translation table
- translate(table)  -> applies a translation table

10. SPLITTING AND JOINING
- split(sep)       -> creates a list by splitting at a separator
- rsplit(sep)      -> splits starting from the right
- splitlines()     -> separates text at line breaks
- join(iterable)   -> combines elements using the calling string
- partition(sep)   -> returns three parts around the first separator
- rpartition(sep) -> returns three parts around the last separator

11. WHITESPACE REMOVAL
- strip()  -> removes leading and trailing characters
- lstrip() -> removes characters from the beginning
- rstrip() -> removes characters from the end

12. ENCODING AND DECODING
- encode() -> converts a string into bytes using an encoding such as UTF-8
- decode() -> converts bytes back into a string

